IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT domain
IMPORT `g2-nouns`

-- g2-art3-4-commissioner.l4: Part 2 of the Charities (Jersey) Law 2014 (Articles 3 and 4): the office of the Jersey Charity Commissioner and its general functions.
-- Group G2.  Imports only prelude, daydate, domain and `g2-nouns`.  No section GIVEN: every input is an ordinary GIVEN, so that an @export reaches nothing it cannot be given.
-- The Commissioner's judgement (what "appears to him or her" reasonably practicable, what is "calculated to facilitate" a function) is an INPUT throughout, as the BRIEF requires.
-- Who holds the office, and who acts for it when the holder cannot (Schedule 1), is not decided here: the role is the constructor `the Commissioner` of `Legal actor`.

§ `Part 2 — Jersey Charity Commissioner`

§§ `Article 3 — Establishment of Jersey Charity Commissioner`

-- src:120 | 3 Establishment of Jersey Charity Commissioner
-- src:121 | (1) There is established a corporation sole to be known as the Jersey Charity Commissioner.
-- src:122 | (2) Schedule 1 makes further provision in respect of the Commissioner.
-- INERT (Article 3, 3(1), 3(2)).  Article 3(1) establishes the office, a corporation sole; its whole effect in this encoding is that the role `the Commissioner` exists, which is the
-- constructor `the Commissioner` of `Legal actor` in domain.l4.  There is no condition to test and no outcome to compute: whether a body "is established" is not a question the Law asks
-- of any caller.  Article 3(2) only points at Schedule 1, which group G9 encodes.  Article 1 defines "Commissioner" as the person for the time being appointed to the office.

§§ `Article 4 — General functions of Commissioner`

-- 4(1) The general functions of the Commissioner.
-- src:124 | (1) The general functions of the Commissioner are –
-- src:125 | (a) to administer the charity test under Part 3, and to operate the charity register under Part 4;
-- src:126 | (b) to supervise the compliance of charity governors with their duties under Part 5;
-- src:127 | (c) to seek to enforce the requirements of Part 6 as to use of terms restricted by or under that Part;
-- src:128 | (d) to publish and maintain guidance on the operation of this Law, including guidance on the duties of governors and guidance on the charity test under Article 5(4);
-- src:129 | (e) to assist other persons (including the Attorney General, the court, the Bailiff, the Comptroller and the JFSC) to discharge, in relation to registered charities and entities with charitable purposes, any function of such a person under any enactment or law, particularly by giving information about registered charities and other entities under Part 7;
-- src:130 | (f) generally to encourage, facilitate and monitor compliance of registered charities with this Law and any enactment under this Law; and
-- src:131 | (g) any other function conferred on the Commissioner by this Law or by any other enactment.
-- INERT (4(1)(a), (b), (c), (e), (f), (g)): each paragraph names a FUNCTION and states no condition and no outcome.  (a) administering the charity test and operating the register is
-- done by the rules of Parts 3 and 4 (groups G2 to G4); (b) supervising governors' compliance, by Part 5 (G6); (c) "seek to enforce" Part 6, which confers no power to prosecute and
-- whose prohibitions are encoded in G7; (e) assisting other persons "particularly by giving information" has no preconditions, and the disclosure rules are Articles 29 to 31 (G8);
-- (f) "generally to encourage, facilitate and monitor" names no act that could be done or omitted; (g) is residual.  What these paragraphs do control is the reach of 4(2),
-- which speaks of "any of his or her functions"; that is carried by the input `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function`.
-- Paragraph (d) is a duty with an act and is encoded below.

-- 4(1)(d) to publish and maintain guidance on the operation of this Law.  The duty names no period, so the rule carries no WITHIN (a deadline would be invented): it stands until performed.
-- "publish" is defined by Article 1 as publishing in a manner that, in the opinion of the person publishing, is likely to bring the information to the attention of the public or of those
-- affected; whether the Commissioner has so published is the Commissioner's opinion and is the event of the trace, not a computation.
-- src:128 | (d) to publish and maintain guidance on the operation of this Law, including guidance on the duties of governors and guidance on the charity test under Article 5(4);
@ref Article 4(1)(d) (src:128)
@export The Commissioner's standing duty under Article 4(1)(d) to publish and maintain guidance on the operation of the Law, including the duties of governors and the charity test; it has no deadline and stays standing until performed
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Articles 3 to 7`
`Art 4(1)(d) the Commissioner must publish and maintain guidance on the operation of the Law` MEANS
    PARTY `the Commissioner`
    MUST `publish and maintain guidance` `the Commissioner` `the operation of the Law, including the duties of governors and the charity test`
    HENCE FULFILLED

-- 4(2) The Commissioner may do anything that is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, the performance of any of his or her functions, other than acting as a
-- governor.  Two inputs, both the Commissioner's or a caller's judgement: whether the thing is so related to a function, and whether it is acting as a governor.
-- src:132 | (2) The Commissioner may do anything (other than acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes) that is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, the performance of any of his or her functions.
@ref Article 4(2) (src:132)
@export Whether the Commissioner has power under Article 4(2) to do a thing: the thing must be calculated to facilitate, or be conducive or incidental to, the performance of a function, and must not be acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes
GIVEN `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, the performance of any of the Commissioner's functions (a judgement; the Law leaves it open)
      `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if the thing the Commissioner proposes to do is to act as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 4(2) the Commissioner has power to do the thing` `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes` MEANS
        NOT `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes`
    AND `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function`

-- src:132 | (2) The Commissioner may do anything (other than acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes) that is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, the performance of any of his or her functions.
@ref Article 4(2) (src:132)
@export The Commissioner's permission under Article 4(2) to do a thing; the permission exists only where Article 4(2) gives the Commissioner power to do it, and an unused permission is not a breach
GIVEN `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, the performance of any of the Commissioner's functions
      `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if the thing the Commissioner proposes to do is to act as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Articles 3 to 7`
`Art 4(2) the Commissioner may do the thing` `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes` MEANS
    IF `Art 4(2) the Commissioner has power to do the thing` `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes`
    THEN PARTY `the Commissioner`
         MAY `do something that is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, the performance of a function` `the Commissioner`
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- 4(3) The Commissioner may in particular, without prejudice to the generality of his or her powers, do three things.  FORK F2.8: whether the examples are bound by the conditions of 4(2)
-- (the first reading, taken) or are powers in their own right (the second).  Nothing in 4(3) says; "in particular" and "without prejudice to the generality" point at illustration.
-- src:133 | (3) The Commissioner may in particular, without prejudice to the generality of his or her powers –
-- src:134 | (a) provide information to the public about the system of registration of charities, including information about the difference between charities and bodies with charitable purposes, information about the advantages of donating to entities that are registered as charities, and information by way of model constitutions;
-- src:135 | (b) advise the Minister as to the nature of charities in Jersey and as to the merits of any proposal for further regulation of charities;
-- src:136 | (c) assist a body in any jurisdiction other than Jersey, that is equivalent to the Commissioner, or to the Attorney General, the court, the Comptroller or the JFSC, in the performance of that body’s functions under the law of that jurisdiction.
@ref Article 4(3) (src:133-136)
@export Whether the Commissioner has power under Article 4(3) to do one of the three listed examples, on the reading of Article 4(3) chosen by the interpretation
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
        @desc The readings of the ambiguous provisions of Articles 3 to 7; the field read here is whether the examples in Article 4(3) add to Article 4(2)
      example IS AN `Example listed in Article 4(3)`
        @desc Which of the three examples in Article 4(3)(a) to (c) the Commissioner proposes to do
      `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, the performance of any of the Commissioner's functions
      `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 4(3) the Commissioner has power to do the listed example` interp example `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes` MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `whether the examples in Article 4(3) add to Article 4(2)`
    WHEN `the examples illustrate Article 4(2) and are bound by its conditions`
        THEN `reading A of Article 4(3): bound by Article 4(2)` `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes`
    WHEN `each example is a power in its own right`
        THEN `reading B of Article 4(3): a power in its own right` example

-- Reading A: an example is a thing the Commissioner may do only where Article 4(2) would allow it.
@ref Article 4(3), Article 4(2) (src:132-136)
GIVEN `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reading A of Article 4(3): bound by Article 4(2)` `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes` MEANS
    `Art 4(2) the Commissioner has power to do the thing` `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes`

-- Reading B: each example is permitted whatever 4(2) says.  None of the three is acting as a governor, so the exclusion of 4(2) has nothing to bite on; the relation to a function is not asked.
@ref Article 4(3) (src:133-136)
GIVEN example IS AN `Example listed in Article 4(3)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reading B of Article 4(3): a power in its own right` example MEANS
    CONSIDER example
    WHEN `providing information to the public about the system of registration of charities` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `advising the Minister about charities in Jersey and further regulation of them` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `assisting an equivalent body outside Jersey in the performance of its functions` THEN TRUE

@ref Article 4(3)(a) to (c) (src:133-136)
GIVEN example IS AN `Example listed in Article 4(3)`
GIVETH AN `Act under Articles 3 to 7`
`the act of the Commissioner that Article 4(3) lists as` example MEANS
    CONSIDER example
    WHEN `providing information to the public about the system of registration of charities`
        THEN `provide information to the public about the system of registration of charities` `the Commissioner`
    WHEN `advising the Minister about charities in Jersey and further regulation of them`
        THEN `advise the Minister about charities in Jersey and further regulation of them` `the Commissioner`
    WHEN `assisting an equivalent body outside Jersey in the performance of its functions`
        THEN `assist an equivalent body outside Jersey in the performance of its functions` `the Commissioner`

-- src:133 | (3) The Commissioner may in particular, without prejudice to the generality of his or her powers –
-- src:134 | (a) provide information to the public about the system of registration of charities, including information about the difference between charities and bodies with charitable purposes, information about the advantages of donating to entities that are registered as charities, and information by way of model constitutions;
-- src:135 | (b) advise the Minister as to the nature of charities in Jersey and as to the merits of any proposal for further regulation of charities;
-- src:136 | (c) assist a body in any jurisdiction other than Jersey, that is equivalent to the Commissioner, or to the Attorney General, the court, the Comptroller or the JFSC, in the performance of that body’s functions under the law of that jurisdiction.
@ref Article 4(3) (src:133-136)
@export The Commissioner's permission under Article 4(3) to do one of the three listed examples, on the reading of Article 4(3) chosen by the interpretation; an unused permission is not a breach
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
        @desc The readings of the ambiguous provisions of Articles 3 to 7; the field read here is whether the examples in Article 4(3) add to Article 4(2)
      example IS AN `Example listed in Article 4(3)`
        @desc Which of the three examples in Article 4(3)(a) to (c) the Commissioner proposes to do
      `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, the performance of any of the Commissioner's functions
      `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes` IS A BOOLEAN
        @desc TRUE if the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Articles 3 to 7`
`Art 4(3) the Commissioner may do the listed example` interp example `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes` MEANS
    IF `Art 4(3) the Commissioner has power to do the listed example` interp example `the thing is calculated to facilitate, or is conducive or incidental to, a function` `the thing is acting as a governor of a charity or of an entity with charitable purposes`
    THEN PARTY `the Commissioner`
         MAY `the act of the Commissioner that Article 4(3) lists as` example
    ELSE FULFILLED

-- 4(4) In performing his or her functions the Commissioner must, so far as appears to him or her reasonably practicable, seek to act in a way that has five limbs: (a) protects public trust
-- and confidence in registered charities and is compatible with the encouragement of (i) all forms of charitable giving and (ii) voluntary participation in the work of registered
-- charities; and (b) is proportionate as to the burdens imposed on, and supports the development of, registered charities.  The source reads "so far appears to him or her", with no "as":
-- a slip in the consolidated text and in the official PDF alike (FRAGMENT-G2.md, section 6).  FORK F2.9: whether the practicability qualification works limb by limb (taken) or
-- governs the whole way of acting at once.
-- src:137 | (4) In performing his or her functions, the Commissioner must, so far appears to him or her to be reasonably practicable, seek to act in a way that –
-- src:138 | (a) protects public trust and confidence in registered charities, and is compatible with the encouragement of –
-- src:139 | (i) all forms of charitable giving, and
-- src:140 | (ii) voluntary participation in the work of registered charities; and
-- src:141 | (b) is proportionate as to the burdens imposed on, and supports the development of registered charities.

-- One limb is met on the first reading when it did not appear reasonably practicable to act in that way, or when the Commissioner sought to.
@ref Article 4(4) (src:137-141)
GIVEN limb IS A `A limb of the standard of conduct in Article 4(4)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reading A of Article 4(4): the limb is met` limb MEANS
    (NOT limb's `it appeared to the Commissioner to be reasonably practicable to act in that way`)
    OR limb's `the Commissioner sought to act in that way`

@ref Article 4(4) (src:137-141)
GIVEN limb IS A `A limb of the standard of conduct in Article 4(4)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 4(4) the limb appeared reasonably practicable to the Commissioner` limb MEANS
    limb's `it appeared to the Commissioner to be reasonably practicable to act in that way`

@ref Article 4(4) (src:137-141)
GIVEN limb IS A `A limb of the standard of conduct in Article 4(4)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 4(4) the Commissioner sought to act in the way the limb describes` limb MEANS
    limb's `the Commissioner sought to act in that way`

@ref Article 4(4) (src:137-141)
GIVEN how IS A `How the Commissioner acted in performing a function`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reading A of Article 4(4): every limb is met so far as it appeared reasonably practicable` how MEANS
        "(a)" ... `reading A of Article 4(4): the limb is met` (how's `protects public trust and confidence in registered charities`)
    AND "(a)(i)" ... `reading A of Article 4(4): the limb is met` (how's `is compatible with the encouragement of all forms of charitable giving`)
    AND "(a)(ii)" ... `reading A of Article 4(4): the limb is met` (how's `is compatible with the encouragement of voluntary participation in the work of registered charities`)
    AND "(b)" ... `reading A of Article 4(4): the limb is met` (how's `is proportionate as to the burdens imposed on registered charities`)
    AND "(b)" ... `reading A of Article 4(4): the limb is met` (how's `supports the development of registered charities`)

@ref Article 4(4) (src:137-141)
GIVEN how IS A `How the Commissioner acted in performing a function`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`it appeared reasonably practicable to the Commissioner to act in the whole way Article 4(4) describes` how MEANS
        `Art 4(4) the limb appeared reasonably practicable to the Commissioner` (how's `protects public trust and confidence in registered charities`)
    AND `Art 4(4) the limb appeared reasonably practicable to the Commissioner` (how's `is compatible with the encouragement of all forms of charitable giving`)
    AND `Art 4(4) the limb appeared reasonably practicable to the Commissioner` (how's `is compatible with the encouragement of voluntary participation in the work of registered charities`)
    AND `Art 4(4) the limb appeared reasonably practicable to the Commissioner` (how's `is proportionate as to the burdens imposed on registered charities`)
    AND `Art 4(4) the limb appeared reasonably practicable to the Commissioner` (how's `supports the development of registered charities`)

@ref Article 4(4) (src:137-141)
GIVEN how IS A `How the Commissioner acted in performing a function`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the Commissioner sought to act in the whole way Article 4(4) describes` how MEANS
        `Art 4(4) the Commissioner sought to act in the way the limb describes` (how's `protects public trust and confidence in registered charities`)
    AND `Art 4(4) the Commissioner sought to act in the way the limb describes` (how's `is compatible with the encouragement of all forms of charitable giving`)
    AND `Art 4(4) the Commissioner sought to act in the way the limb describes` (how's `is compatible with the encouragement of voluntary participation in the work of registered charities`)
    AND `Art 4(4) the Commissioner sought to act in the way the limb describes` (how's `is proportionate as to the burdens imposed on registered charities`)
    AND `Art 4(4) the Commissioner sought to act in the way the limb describes` (how's `supports the development of registered charities`)

-- Reading B: the standard binds only if it appeared reasonably practicable to act in the whole way, and then every limb must have been sought.
@ref Article 4(4) (src:137-141)
GIVEN how IS A `How the Commissioner acted in performing a function`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`reading B of Article 4(4): the whole way, if it appeared reasonably practicable` how MEANS
    (NOT `it appeared reasonably practicable to the Commissioner to act in the whole way Article 4(4) describes` how)
    OR `the Commissioner sought to act in the whole way Article 4(4) describes` how

-- src:137 | (4) In performing his or her functions, the Commissioner must, so far appears to him or her to be reasonably practicable, seek to act in a way that –
-- src:138 | (a) protects public trust and confidence in registered charities, and is compatible with the encouragement of –
-- src:139 | (i) all forms of charitable giving, and
-- src:140 | (ii) voluntary participation in the work of registered charities; and
-- src:141 | (b) is proportionate as to the burdens imposed on, and supports the development of registered charities.
@ref Article 4(4) (src:137-141)
@export Whether the Commissioner has sought to act as Article 4(4) requires in performing a function, on the reading of the practicability qualification chosen by the interpretation
GIVEN interp IS AN `Interpretation of Articles 3 to 7`
        @desc The readings of the ambiguous provisions of Articles 3 to 7; the field read here is how the practicability qualification of Article 4(4) works
      how IS A `How the Commissioner acted in performing a function`
        @desc For each of the five limbs of Article 4(4): whether it appeared to the Commissioner reasonably practicable to act in that way, and whether the Commissioner sought to
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Art 4(4) the Commissioner has sought to act as Article 4(4) requires` interp how MEANS
    CONSIDER interp's `how so far as reasonably practicable qualifies Article 4(4)`
    WHEN `each limb binds so far as it appeared reasonably practicable`
        THEN `reading A of Article 4(4): every limb is met so far as it appeared reasonably practicable` how
    WHEN `the standard binds only if it appeared reasonably practicable to act in the whole way`
        THEN `reading B of Article 4(4): the whole way, if it appeared reasonably practicable` how

-- The duty itself, as a standing obligation: it has no period (it attaches to each performance of a function) and the act is a state of seeking, so the trace records that the
-- Commissioner did seek to act in the way Article 4(4) describes.  Whether the Commissioner did so is the boolean above; a trace cannot judge limbs.
-- src:137 | (4) In performing his or her functions, the Commissioner must, so far appears to him or her to be reasonably practicable, seek to act in a way that –
@ref Article 4(4) (src:137)
@export The Commissioner's standing duty under Article 4(4) to seek to act, in performing a function, in a way that protects public trust and confidence in registered charities and is proportionate; it has no deadline
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Legal actor` `Act under Articles 3 to 7`
`Art 4(4) the Commissioner must seek to act as Article 4(4) requires` MEANS
    PARTY `the Commissioner`
    MUST `seek to act in a way that protects public trust and confidence in registered charities and is proportionate` `the Commissioner`
    HENCE FULFILLED
